# shir_nodes/fs_command.node — a filesystem COMMAND as one semantic node.
#
# `rm -f a b`, `mkdir -p d`, `touch f` are the corpus's file-mutating
# externals. Their renderer carries semantics that are easy to get wrong (the
# directory rule, the `-f` missing-path rule, bash's diagnostic wording, the
# status aggregated over several operands), so they belong in a node instead of
# being re-derived by each backend.
#
# meaning: fs_command cmd argv == run `cmd argv...` in the enclosing shell,
#   with `cmd` one of `rm`, `mkdir`, `touch`, leaving its status in `$?`.
#
# Domains this node deliberately does NOT cover (the producer refuses them, so
# the command keeps whatever route it had):
#   * a word that is not a plain literal at IR time (a variable, a
#     substitution, a GLOB — `rm file_*.txt` is one `Str` at IR time and N
#     paths at runtime, so it is a refusal and never an arm that drops it).
#     THE ONE EXCEPTION is `__ps_tmp*`: process substitution's own scratch
#     reads, minted by the native mktemp block and therefore always exactly
#     one literal path (never a glob, never several words, never empty). Its
#     late-appended cleanup `rm -f $__ps_tmp0 …` is lifted (by
#     `process_subst::rm_exec`, re-applying this transform) so that the
#     transform deletes its own temp files natively instead of forking bash;
#   * a flag the renderer does not model (`rm -i`, `mkdir -m`, `touch -d`, any
#     `--long` form);
#   * `rm`/`mkdir`/`touch` with no operand, or a lone `-` operand.
#
# Backends: Rust (its statement renderer spells the `std::fs` loop). Every
# other backend refuses the tag in its manifest, so the producer never builds
# it there.
# Fallback: none — the node exists to replace the shell-out, not to keep it.
#
# Provenance: PLAN.md pass 9 (the fs arms) and pass 12 (this family).
node FsCommand
tag "FsCommand"
kind expr
# The command word: "rm" | "mkdir" | "touch".
field cmd: string
# The argv, as one Array expr of literal Str words (flags and operands in
# shell order).
field argv: expr
spec: FsCommands#command_sound
